 "topics": [
  {
   "topic": "Missing-identity disposition API, granularity, scope, caller selection, and validation",
   "members": [
    "r1.01",
    "r2.00",
    "r3.02",
    "r3.03",
    "r3.04"
   ],
   "runs": [
    1,
    2,
    3
   ],
   "questions": [
    "What explicit input selects the missing-identity disposition, is it one disposition for the whole identity read or one per identity, and which callers pass each value?",
    "How does an identity read name its missing-identity policy, is the policy one value for the whole call or one per identity, and which callers must select omission, `unknown_reference`, or `internal_error`?",
    "What explicit input carries the missing-identity disposition, and is it available only for identity reads or also for relations?",
    "If one batch contains identities of different provenance, does one disposition govern the whole call or does each identity carry its own disposition?",
    "Which module chooses `omitted`, `unknown_reference`, or `internal_error` at each call site, and how is a caller prevented from choosing omission for a required stored reference or `unknown_reference` for a store defect?"
   ],
   "units": [
    "50_public_apis.md:Conventions every operation follows",
    "50_public_apis.md:`public_op:store.read_records`"
   ],
   "id": "T1",
   "judgement": {
    "kind": "judged_before",
    "quotes": [],
    "later_state": null,
    "divergence": "",
    "why": "This is the same question about the missing-identity policy input, granularity, scope, and caller selection already decided by P1.",
    "precedent": {
     "id": "P1",
     "round": "round-114",
     "topic": "Input and granularity of the missing-identity policy for store.read_records",
     "kind": "answered_later",
     "quotes": [
      "Every identity query — by_id, by_position, by_attempt and spooled_files — carries when_missing (= models.MissingRecord), and the store alone decides absence by it: omit answers the records it finds and omits an identity no record has; unknown_reference raises UnknownReferenceError naming the first identity no record has, in the order given, and answers nothing; internal_error raises StoreInternalError naming it the same way. A caller passes unknown_reference for an identity its own caller named, internal_error for an identity a stored record names (a reference the store must hold), and omit only when it asks whether the record exists; it never checks the answer for absence itself."
     ],
     "why": "State 6 defines one `when_missing` field on every identity query and makes its value apply to the query's missing identities."
    },
    "verified": true,
    "blocking": false
   }
  },
  {
   "topic": "Outcome for missing relation arguments obtained from stored records",
   "members": [
    "r2.01",
    "r3.01"
   ],
   "runs": [
    2,
    3
   ],
   "questions": [
    "When a relation argument was obtained from a stored record rather than named by the external request, does its absence produce `unknown_reference` as the relation rule says or `internal_error` as the convention says?",
    "When a relation argument was obtained from another stored record and is missing, must the relation return `internal_error` under the new convention or `unknown_reference` under the relation rule?"
   ],
   "units": [
    "50_public_apis.md:Relations of `store.read_records`"
   ],
   "id": "T2",
   "judgement": {
    "kind": "contradiction",
    "quotes": [
     "A missing record a request names is `unknown_reference` naming it, whatever operation looked it up; a record that another stored record names and the store cannot find is a defect, `internal_error` (decision 30).",
     "The record a relation\nnames — every one of them — must exist, or the call is\n`unknown_reference`, naming the first missing record in the order the\nrelation's name mentions them (A16 rule 1: checks in the order listed); from an existing\nrecord, an empty answer is a result, not a refusal."
    ],
    "later_state": null,
    "divergence": "",
    "why": "The first passage makes a missing relation argument obtained from a stored record an internal error, while the second makes every missing record named by a relation an unknown reference.",
    "verified": true,
    "blocking": true
   }
  },
  {
   "topic": "Selecting missing-record outcomes based on request versus stored-reference provenance",
   "members": [
    "r1.02",
    "r3.05"
   ],
   "runs": [
    1,
    3
   ],
   "questions": [
    "How does `read_value_bytes` distinguish a request-supplied identity, which may be `unknown_reference`, from an identity obtained from a stored record, which the new convention makes `internal_error`?",
    "For each operation that can receive either a request-named identity or an identity taken from a stored record, how is the missing-record outcome selected?"
   ],
   "units": [
    "50_public_apis.md:Conventions every operation follows"
   ],
   "id": "T3",
   "judgement": {
    "kind": "contradiction",
    "quotes": [
     "A missing record a request names is `unknown_reference` naming it, whatever operation looked it up; a record that another stored record names and the store cannot find is a defect, `internal_error` (decision 30).",
     "`unknown_reference` for an identity naming no record; `internal_error` when the\nbytes of a record that should hold them are missing, unreadable or fail their\ndigest."
    ],
    "later_state": null,
    "divergence": "",
    "why": "The convention classifies a missing record reached through another stored record as internal_error, but read_value_bytes classifies every identity naming no record as unknown_reference.",
    "verified": true,
    "blocking": true
   }
  },
  {
   "topic": "Treatment of absent contract versions and bindings referenced by a stored FlowVersion",
   "members": [
    "r1.00",
    "r2.02"
   ],
   "runs": [
    1,
    2
   ],
   "questions": [
    "Are unknown contract versions and bindings stored inside a composed FlowVersion intentional proof failures, or store defects reported as `internal_error`?",
    "Are contract-version and binding identities stored inside an unproven FlowVersion allowed to be absent and reported as a normal phase-1 proof failure, or must their absence now be treated as store corruption and `internal_error`?"
   ],
   "units": [
    "50_public_apis.md:Conventions every operation follows"
   ],
   "id": "T4",
   "judgement": {
    "kind": "contradiction",
    "quotes": [
     "A missing record a request names is `unknown_reference` naming it, whatever operation looked it up; a record that another stored record names and the store cannot find is a defect, `internal_error` (decision 30).",
     "a contract version or binding named inside a composed flow\nversion's content is a phase 1 proof failure (A05); refusal codes are State\n5's.",
     "State 0's \"kept either way\" is about the proof: a version is kept whether or\n   not it is proven. A malformed request is not a version."
    ],
    "later_state": null,
    "divergence": "",
    "why": "A stored FlowVersion may intentionally retain absent contract-version or binding identities for a normal phase-1 proof failure, while the general convention calls any missing record named by another stored record an internal error.",
    "verified": true,
    "blocking": true
   }
  },
